Programs need to make decisions based on changing data values. In Python, this is achieved using conditional blocks. The interpreter evaluates an expression to either True or False:
temperature = 22

if temperature > 30:
    print("It's hot outside.")
elif temperature > 15:
    print("The weather is pleasant.")
else:
    print("It's cold outside.")
Python relies heavily on strict indentation (usually 4 spaces) to define which lines of code belong inside each execution block.

The backend tier handles requests coming from incoming web browsers, communicates with databases, and sends back responses. Using Express.js (a minimalist framework for Node.js), defining an endpoint route looks like this:

const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/api/users', (req, res) => {
    const users = [{ id: 1, name: "Alice" }, { id: 2, name: "Bob" }];
    res.json(users);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'));

The code above listens for incoming HTTP GET requests at the /api/users path and responds by delivering user data cleanly formatted as JSON strings.
